          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You learned this in your first chapter: a noun ending in a vowel adds -s. So what is \"houses\"? (Casas.) Now — what is \"the houses\"?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the article does what the noun does",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "las — the, for more than one feminine thing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la casa becomes las casas — the houses. la comida becomes las comidas — the meals."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The -s lands on both words. That is not two rules; it is one rule applied where it belongs. You have already seen Spanish do this — buenas noches is buena copying noche's number — and it is the same instinct throughout the language: the words around a noun agree with it, so when the noun goes plural, they follow."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So las is not a word to memorise. It is la obeying the rule you already hold."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
